## 84 recorded 83: fact Rpc "{\"id\":\"amux-prompt-70312d34\",\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"result\":{\"stopReason\"…
item message:2 message final at=3224 text=" times every night. His boots made no sound against the metal; after thirty years on the ragged spit called Crow’s Tooth, he moved with the hush of an owl.\n\nAt the summit, inside the lantern room, the world opened into blackness and salt spray. The wind shrieked against the curved storm panes, rattling the bronze frames with the frantic energy of something desperate to get inside. Arthur paid it no heed. He struck a match—shielding the fragile spark with cupped, calloused hands—and touched it to the brass burner.\n\nLight bloomed. The great Fresnel lens, taller than a man and carved with concentric rings of heavy French glass, caught the flame and magnified it tenfold, a hundredfold, until a blade of pure, sharp amber thrust outward into the boiling dark.\n\nArthur checked the clockwork weights that kept the lens turning. They were descending smoothly through the center column of the tower. Satisfied, he stepped onto the narrow catwalk that ringed the lantern room, leaning his elbows against the iron railing.\n\nBelow, the Atlantic was a fury of black slate and froth. Out there, invisible beyond the spray, ships were running blind, tossed like woodchips across shoals that could tear an oak keel to kindling. To those men, Arthur was not a person; he was an eye. He was a promise that the earth still existed, fixed and unyielding.\n\nHe reached into his woolen coat and pulled out a small, pocket-worn notebook. He recorded the hour, the barometric pressure, the gale force, and the rotation rate of the lens. Routine was the anchor that kept a keeper from drifting away into the solitude. People in town often asked him if he grew lonely out here, six miles from the mainland, with only screaming gulls and the thrum of the foghorn for company.\n\nHe never knew how to explain that solitude here felt vast, not empty. Out on the rock, stripped of human gossip and trivial noise, life was reduced to the clean, elemental terms of darkness and light, danger and salvation.\n\nSuddenly, through the sweep of the beacon, he caught a flash of white canvas—a schooner, dangerously close to the sunken reefs of the outer shoals. Arthur’s heart seized. He watched the beam swing across the dark water, illuminating the vessel just long enough to reveal its listing mast and the panic on its deck.\n\n*Turn,* Arthur willed across the gale, gripping the railing until his knuckles went pale. *See the light. Bear north.*\n\nThe beacon swung around again. The vessel had caught the signal. Its helm responded, carving hard against the white foam, skirting the black teeth of the reef by mere yards before slipping into the safety of deep water.\n\nArthur let out a breath he had not realized he was holding. He wiped salt brine from his beard, adjusted the wick, and began his long descent down the seventy-four steps to wait for dawn.\n\nsteered"
